  "summary": "Mexican strawberry shipments are under US investigation on antidumping grounds, with timing sensitivity tied to the US election cycle. The Mexican civil aviation authority will undergo US FAA assessment this week on safety standards compliance, with potential consequences for Mexico's aviation category ranking. Official gazette publication of reforms to Mexico's Manual de Contabilidad Gubernamental establishes new accounting and reporting standards for federal entities.",
  "lead": {
    "h1": "Mexican strawberry exports face US antidumping investigation ahead of US elections",
    "context": "Mexican strawberry shipments are under US investigation on antidumping grounds, with timing sensitivity tied to the US election cycle.",
    "background": "The US Commerce Department opened the case in February. Its official notice describes 18.32% as the alleged dumping margin, not a final duty.",
    "view": "The immediate risk is uncertainty for growers, not a proven 18.32% tariff. The claim that election politics drove the case comes from Mexico's farm council. The official record shows Florida producers filed the petition, but it does not establish their motive.",
    "prediction": "Watch Commerce case A-201-869 for its next determination. A finding that sets an actual duty would turn the allegation into a trade cost. A lower or zero margin would weaken the cost concern.",
    "analysisV": 9,
